WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
the european union is reportedly  planning to sanction 10 individuals and  two companies  connected to the military regime in  myanmar according to news agency reuters  the sanctions could come into effect by  next week  the sanctions come amid a warning from  the u.n human rights chief  who said the situation in the country  could be spiraling  to a full-blown syria style conflict  more than 700 people have been killed  since the military  took power in a coup in february and  since then  its crackdown on pro-democracy  protesters has only grown  stronger these are the lengths myanmar's  military will go to  to crack down on dissenting voices  this video from social media shows the  moment a prominent protest leader is  deliberately  rammed off his motorbike  waymo nang was arrested by armed men  after trying to flee the incident  with friends saying they now fear for  his safety  security forces have used increasingly  violent tactics to quell the anti-coup  movement  the un says there are echoes of the  civil war in syria  there too we saw peaceful protests met  with unnecessary and clearly  disproportionate force  the state's brutal persistent repression  of its own people  led to some individuals taking up arms  followed by a downward  and rapidly expanding spiral of violence  all across the country  despite the risk to their lives  protesters have been trying to keep up  the pressure on the military regime  the spilled red paint symbolizes the  blood of those who've died  activists say at least 700 people have  been killed since protests began in  february  normally at the moment people in myanmar  would be celebrating tanjan  a buddhist new year holiday but not this  year  it's important for young people to rebel  against a dictator  we can't be happy now as long as we live  under this dictatorship our future is  lost  the protesters aren't giving up but  neither it seems  is the military  phil robertson from human rights watch  has been monitoring the situation in  myanmar  for the past couple of months he joins  me now from bangkok phil  have we entered a phase in myanmar where  the junta is comfortable with the status  quo  and relatively confident of keeping  power  well i'm not sure that they're confident  yet but they are certainly acting on a  presumption that the protesters on the  streets  are their enemy and they're using lethal  force against them  uh you know they're treating myanmar  cities like a battlefield and  they've used machine guns and grenades  and rpgs against protesters in places  like bago  where last week we saw more than 80  people killed in one morning  now what are you hearing from protesters  on the ground do they feel  the international community standing up  for them  they are disappointed by the  international community they want to see  more action  they appreciate all the statements that  have been made by various foreign  ministers around the world and by the  u.n security council and others  but they're calling for much more i i  think if they had their way  uh they would see an invocation by the  u.n security council of the  responsibility to protect  doctrine and some sort of deployment uh  by the international community into  myanmar but i don't think that's  realistic and i don't think that's  really  in anybody's plans uh you know whether  it be the us eu or elsewhere  so you know the people i think are  disappointed the international community  hasn't come in as almost like the  cavalry but  they're determined to continue  protesting uh they're angry  at the military and they are uh quite  clear that they do not want this  myanmar military in any sort of future  for myanmar  and while we are talking phil there are  also reports that the eu may further  sanction officials and companies  connected to the junta now these aren't  going to be the first sanctions from the  eu  but is this going to help change the  situation  in any way  well it's a substantive step forward the  european union has frankly been a bit  slow  in imposing uh targeted economic  sanctions  now we're hearing that they're going to  go after uh the two major  military-controlled business  conglomerates uh the union myanmar  economic holdings and uh the myanmar  economic corporation which is  what we have been asking them to do from  the very beginning  we have to go after the companies we  have to go after the cash flow  to the myanmar military junta just  targeting  top level myanmar military officials  it's important but it's not enough  speaking of the economic side of things  i mean myanmar's main trading partners  in the region are countries like china  india japan  thailand for example do you think these  countries are doing enough  to enable a return to democracy  quite clearly the frontline states are  not doing enough to  actually uh pressure the myanmar  military junta  to return to the barracks and to allow  for the democracy that they have  destroyed to  uh be reconstituted i i think that  unfortunately we're seeing uh go along  to get along sort of arrangement  from these frontline states like  thailand and india  and now there's hope that somehow there  will be some action  from the uh asean the association of  southeast asian nations  but we're not seeing that we're just  seeing a very divided  regional body here that's not really  prepared to go forward in unity to  pressure the government in myanmar we  leave it  there for the time being but thank you  so much for joining us phil robertson  from human rights watch  thank you now the military's  violent crackdown against protesters is  forcing many to seek shelter  with armed ethnic groups that have been  for years  fighting the army groups such as the  karen national liberation army based in  southeastern cayenne state  on the border with thailand  the ethnic karen like many other ethnic  groups in the country have been  four years fighting the military for  some form of self-government  they are now proving life savers for  those fleeing the military's crackdown  on democracy it may not look like  much but this rural forest camp has  become a sanctuary  for myanmar's terrified protesters  although we had been protesting  peacefully we had to run away because of  the violent crackdown  we knew that if we were to get arrested  the security forces would not let us  live  they would kill us  they arrived in the territory of one of  the country's key armed rebel groups  the karan national liberation army the  group controls large waves of land on  the border with thailand  and has been fighting the central  government for the last 70 years  life in the camp is spartan among its  new inhabitants are striking teachers  who say they had no choice but to flee  there  after the junta issued warrants for  their arrests  there is now trust between the civil  servants and the ethnic armed groups  they help us and they're kind we feel  safe being here  since they started siding with us we  told them ask us for anything that we  can do to help  we are ready to help this new  partnership has brought renewed  attention to the broad spectrum of  resistance that the junta now faces  and raised concerns that should they go  on the offensive  violence could spiral out of control  and there's reports now of members of  ethnic groups uniting with ousted  lawmakers and anti-q protesters  to form a national unity government in  the country  something we'll be tracking in the  coming weeks

